1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:06,602
For three hours, a Boeing seven three seven flew over Greece with every single person on board already unconscious.

2
00:00:07,733 --> 00:00:12,910
The cause traced back to a single switch, left in the wrong position, hours before takeoff.

3
00:00:12,910 --> 00:00:16,470
Investigators found the crew had three separate chances to catch it.

4
00:00:16,470 --> 00:00:17,764
They missed every one.

5
00:00:18,899 --> 00:00:24,297
One flight attendant, holding a real pilot's license of his own, made it into the cockpit before the end.

6
00:00:24,297 --> 00:00:27,990
What he found there is on the record, in his own mayday call.

7
00:00:29,133 --> 00:00:31,488
One honest rule before we start.

8
00:00:31,488 --> 00:00:39,731
Every quote, timestamp, and finding in this episode comes from the official Greek accident investigation report, or from real court records.

9
00:00:39,731 --> 00:00:44,049
Where something is disputed or unproven, we'll say so, on screen.

10
00:00:45,166 --> 00:00:47,204
One-twenty-five in the morning.

11
00:00:48,333 --> 00:00:55,094
A Boeing seven three seven, tail number five bravo dee bee why, lands at Larnaca after a flight in from London Heathrow.

12
00:00:55,094 --> 00:00:58,782
The cabin crew log a problem before anyone even leaves the aircraft.

13
00:00:59,899 --> 00:01:02,021
A ground engineer is called out.

14
00:01:02,021 --> 00:01:05,910
Forty four years old, a freelance contractor, licensed in the UK.

15
00:01:05,910 --> 00:01:10,506
He inspects the door, and runs a manual pressurization leak check by hand.

16
00:01:11,633 --> 00:01:15,708
At three fifteen A M, he signs the aircraft back into service.

17
00:01:15,708 --> 00:01:19,443
His written log reads: nil defects. No leaks. No abnormal noises.

18
00:01:19,443 --> 00:01:23,179
The plane is cleared to fly again in a few hours.

19
00:01:24,299 --> 00:01:28,795
The official investigation would later find this was not a documented failure of his duty.

20
00:01:28,795 --> 00:01:31,493
It was one part of a much longer chain.

21
00:01:31,493 --> 00:01:35,688
But at three fifteen that morning, nobody yet knows that chain has already started.

22
00:01:36,833 --> 00:01:41,509
To understand what happened next, you have to understand the airline flying that plane.

23
00:01:42,633 --> 00:01:46,677
Helios Airways was Cyprus's first privately owned airline, established in 1999.

24
00:01:46,677 --> 00:01:52,191
Its own mission statement, on the record, was to be a profitable, low fares airline.

25
00:01:52,191 --> 00:01:57,706
Its managing director once put it more bluntly: to fly at probably half the price.

26
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:05,096
By 2004, the airline's owner had acquired it specifically to feed passengers into his own tour company.

27
00:02:05,096 --> 00:02:09,517
British aviation regulators audited Helios repeatedly in the years before the crash.

28
00:02:10,666 --> 00:02:15,870
Real findings, on the record: no proper in-house quality system. Outdated manuals.

29
00:02:15,870 --> 00:02:18,038
Incomplete training files. Understaffed management.

30
00:02:18,038 --> 00:02:23,675
In November 2004, the airline came close to losing its operating certificate altogether.

31
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:29,868
This is the airline, and the culture, behind the aircraft that landed at Larnaca that night in August.

32
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,847
The aircraft itself already had a history.

33
00:02:34,966 --> 00:02:40,897
In December of 2004, this same plane suffered a sudden decompression on a completely different flight.

34
00:02:40,897 --> 00:02:45,344
A hole opened in a door seal. Three passengers needed hospital treatment.

35
00:02:45,344 --> 00:02:48,680
Investigators at the time could never conclusively determine why.

36
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:58,087
Between June and the second week of August 2005, the same plane racked up nine separate technical write ups for its cooling system.

37
00:02:58,087 --> 00:03:00,249
Nine. In just over two months.

38
00:03:01,366 --> 00:03:03,883
None of this, on its own, grounded the aircraft.

39
00:03:03,883 --> 00:03:08,915
All of it, together, is the plane that took off from Larnaca at nine oh seven that morning.

40
00:03:10,033 --> 00:03:13,664
Five minutes after leaving the ground, the first alarm sounds.

41
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:21,301
At nine twelve and thirty eight seconds, climbing through twelve thousand feet, a warning horn sounds in the cockpit.

42
00:03:21,301 --> 00:03:25,066
It is the cabin altitude warning. The plane is losing pressure.

43
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:29,727
Here is the design flaw nobody in that cockpit knew about.

44
00:03:29,727 --> 00:03:37,746
On this aircraft, the cabin altitude horn sounds exactly like a completely different warning — one that only ever matters on the ground, before takeoff.

45
00:03:38,866 --> 00:03:41,627
Two minutes later, the captain radios the ground.

46
00:03:41,627 --> 00:03:49,564
His own words, on the record: the takeoff configuration warning is on, and the cooling equipment is showing normal and alternate off line.

47
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:57,323
He has misread the one warning that could have saved every life on that plane, as a completely different, harmless one.

48
00:03:57,323 --> 00:04:03,631
On the same call, the ground engineer from that night asks him directly: is the pressurization panel set to automatic?

49
00:04:04,766 --> 00:04:09,378
The captain, already beginning to lose oxygen to his brain, doesn't answer it.

50
00:04:09,378 --> 00:04:11,507
He asks about circuit breakers instead.

51
00:04:11,507 --> 00:04:15,764
It is the last transmission anyone ever hears from Helios flight 522.

52
00:04:16,899 --> 00:04:18,780
The aircraft keeps climbing.

53
00:04:19,899 --> 00:04:29,026
By nine twenty three, it levels off at its programmed cruising altitude, thirty four thousand feet, and keeps flying its planned route.

54
00:04:29,026 --> 00:04:30,271
On autopilot. Alone.

55
00:04:31,399 --> 00:04:36,557
At nine thirty seven, it enters Greek airspace with no one answering the radio.

56
00:04:36,557 --> 00:04:41,347
Air traffic control tries to reach it again, and again. Nineteen separate attempts.

57
00:04:41,347 --> 00:04:42,084
Nobody answers.

58
00:04:43,233 --> 00:04:47,308
With no instructions, the autopilot does exactly what it was programmed to do.

59
00:04:47,308 --> 00:04:52,324
It enters a holding pattern over a radio beacon near Athens, and starts flying in circles.

60
00:04:53,466 --> 00:04:58,027
At eleven oh five, two F-16 fighter jets scramble from a Greek air base.

61
00:04:58,027 --> 00:04:59,982
Their job is simply to look.

62
00:04:59,982 --> 00:05:05,195
To find out, visually, what is happening on a passenger jet that will not answer anyone.

63
00:05:06,333 --> 00:05:10,227
On its sixth loop of the holding pattern, they catch up to it.

64
00:05:10,227 --> 00:05:15,319
What they see, through the cockpit windows, becomes some of the most quoted testimony in aviation history.

65
00:05:16,466 --> 00:05:21,054
The first officer's seat: slumped forward, motionless. The captain's seat: empty.

66
00:05:21,054 --> 00:05:26,477
Oxygen masks, dangling loose in the cabin behind them. No visible movement, anywhere.

67
00:05:26,477 --> 00:05:28,979
No sign of anything but silence.

68
00:05:30,099 --> 00:05:32,685
Everyone on that plane was not unconscious.

69
00:05:33,833 --> 00:05:42,424
A twenty five year old flight attendant, Andreas Prodromou, held something almost nobody in his job does: a real, valid UK commercial pilot's license.

70
00:05:42,424 --> 00:05:47,077
Not a rating for this aircraft. But real flight training, all the same.

71
00:05:48,199 --> 00:05:53,014
At eleven forty nine, using a portable oxygen bottle, he makes it into the cockpit.

72
00:05:53,014 --> 00:05:57,186
He sits in the captain's now empty seat. The F-16 pilots see him.

73
00:05:57,186 --> 00:05:59,432
He briefly raises a hand toward them.

74
00:06:00,566 --> 00:06:04,879
Seconds later, the left engine flames out. The plane has run out of fuel.

75
00:06:04,879 --> 00:06:07,959
It leaves its holding pattern, and starts to come down.

76
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:13,057
The cockpit voice recorder captures five separate mayday calls from Prodromou.

77
00:06:13,057 --> 00:06:17,014
Nobody at Athens air traffic control ever hears a single one.

78
00:06:17,014 --> 00:06:21,691
His radio is still tuned to Larnaca's frequency, hundreds of miles behind him.

79
00:06:22,833 --> 00:06:27,281
Mayday, mayday, mayday. Helios airways flight five two two, Athens.

80
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:31,435
At eleven fifty nine, the second engine flames out.

81
00:06:31,435 --> 00:06:39,868
At twelve oh three and thirty two seconds, the aircraft comes down in the hills near a town called Grammatiko, twenty five miles from Athens.

82
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:46,858
121 people were aboard. 115 passengers. 6 crew. All 121 were killed.

83
00:06:46,858 --> 00:06:48,811
There were no survivors.

84
00:06:49,933 --> 00:06:52,780
The official investigation ran for over a year.

85
00:06:53,900 --> 00:07:00,236
Greece's Air Accident Investigation and Aviation Safety Board published its final findings in October of 2006.

86
00:07:00,236 --> 00:07:06,176
Its stated cause, in order: the crew never recognized the pressurization switch was in manual.

87
00:07:06,176 --> 00:07:08,552
They never identified the real problem.

88
00:07:08,552 --> 00:07:13,701
And one by one, everyone but Prodromou lost consciousness to lack of oxygen.

89
00:07:14,833 --> 00:07:18,110
The report is careful, and specific, about something important.

90
00:07:18,110 --> 00:07:23,208
It does not pin this on one person. The switch. The identical warning horns.

91
00:07:23,208 --> 00:07:28,306
Boeing's own maintenance manual, which never explicitly told anyone to double check that switch.

92
00:07:28,306 --> 00:07:33,040
And a flight crew who missed three separate chances to catch it themselves.

93
00:07:34,166 --> 00:07:45,712
One real, direct result: years later, air safety regulators ordered older 737s to be fitted with two distinct warning lights, so this exact confusion could never happen again.

94
00:07:46,833 --> 00:07:49,602
The technical report was never the end of this story.

95
00:07:50,733 --> 00:07:56,136
Two separate criminal cases followed, in two different countries, against some of the same people.

96
00:07:56,136 --> 00:07:59,377
In Cyprus, four Helios executives stood trial for manslaughter.

97
00:07:59,377 --> 00:08:02,619
In December of 2011, a court acquitted all four.

98
00:08:03,766 --> 00:08:07,117
Prosecutors appealed. Cyprus's Supreme Court voted to order a retrial.

99
00:08:07,117 --> 00:08:13,483
That retrial never happened — the same men were already being tried again, in Greece, for the same crash.

100
00:08:14,633 --> 00:08:22,353
In Greece, three of those men — the airline's CEO, its operations manager, and its former chief pilot — were convicted of manslaughter.

101
00:08:22,353 --> 00:08:23,360
Ten year sentences.

102
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:28,394
Under Greek law, all three were allowed to pay their way out of prison instead.

103
00:08:28,394 --> 00:08:32,422
Roughly eighty thousand euros each. None of them served a single day.

104
00:08:33,566 --> 00:08:38,258
The ground engineer who left that switch in manual was also tried in Greece.

105
00:08:38,258 --> 00:08:39,933
He was acquitted, on appeal.

106
00:08:39,933 --> 00:08:45,295
Of everyone charged in either country, he is the only person fully cleared of criminal responsibility.

107
00:08:46,433 --> 00:08:49,829
Helios Airways itself did not survive long, either.

108
00:08:50,966 --> 00:08:54,389
It kept flying for just days after the crash.

109
00:08:54,389 --> 00:08:58,573
In March of 2006, it rebranded entirely, dropping its old name.

110
00:08:58,573 --> 00:09:03,897
By that November, roughly fourteen months after the crash, it had stopped operating completely.

111
00:09:05,033 --> 00:09:12,363
A monument and a small chapel stand today at the crash site near Grammatiko, bearing all 121 names and photographs.

112
00:09:12,363 --> 00:09:16,762
Every year, families gather there, and separately in Cyprus, to remember them.

113
00:09:17,900 --> 00:09:23,904
As recently as August of last year — twenty years after the crash — families were still speaking publicly.

114
00:09:23,904 --> 00:09:27,696
Their own words, on the record: that real justice was never served.

115
00:09:28,833 --> 00:09:32,443
A switch, left in one position instead of another.

116
00:09:32,443 --> 00:09:37,256
Two warning systems that sounded identical. Three missed chances to catch it.

117
00:09:37,256 --> 00:09:41,267
That is the entire, real chain that ended 121 lives.

118
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:45,560
Nobody involved set out that morning to cause any of this.

119
00:09:45,560 --> 00:09:49,009
That is, honestly, the hardest part of this story to sit with.

120
00:09:50,133 --> 00:09:58,769
If a switch left in the wrong position can end a flight like this, wait until you see what one overlooked repair did to a different Boeing, seven years later.

121
00:09:58,769 --> 00:10:00,784
That story is on this channel too.

122
00:10:00,784 --> 00:10:04,814
Subscribe to Black Box Breakdown, so you don't miss what evidence turns up next.

